# Feature workflow

1. Capture or select exactly one feature.
   - For a direct request, run `python scripts/feature_registry.py new "TITLE"`, faithfully fill its
     specification from the request, then mark it `READY` and run `sync` when requirements are
     sufficient. Do not invent major requirements. If an essential decision is missing, ask the
     user or mark it `BLOCKED`, record the decision needed, sync, and stop.
   - For existing work, run `python scripts/feature_registry.py next-ready`, select only the returned
     ID, and stop on `NO_READY_FEATURE`.
2. Understand the selected specification and acceptance criteria. Read only that feature, not the
   whole registry.
3. Localize the current implementation and relevant tests by searching before broad reading.
4. Assess callers, data flow, dependencies, and documentation impact.
{% if profile == "minimal" -%}
5. Identify the smallest owning module; introduce a new boundary only when the feature needs it.
{% else -%}
5. Identify the owning layer or domain before editing; preserve the **{{ profile }}** dependency
   direction.
{% endif -%}
6. Define verification before implementation.
7. Mark the feature `IN_PROGRESS` and run `sync` before editing implementation code.
8. Implement the minimum coherent change without speculative layers or dependencies.
9. Run the targeted behavior test, then the required repository gates.
10. Review the final diff for scope, ownership, and accidental changes.
11. Update the implementation record with result, files, dependencies, verification, important
    decisions, deviations, and follow-up candidates. Do not implement follow-ups opportunistically.
12. Mark the feature `DONE` only after its acceptance criteria and gates pass, run `sync`, run
    `check`, and stop. Do not automatically begin another feature.

At any step, if work becomes externally blocked, mark the feature `BLOCKED`, record the exact
blocker and smallest next action, run `sync`, and stop.
